From ueb0a34481312005c at groups.google.com  Tue Apr 18 17:06:24 2017
From: ueb0a34481312005c at groups.google.com (msquatrito)
Date: Tue, 18 Apr 2017 17:06:24 +0000
Subject: dplyr_0.5.0.9002, passing string as variable name to select
Message-ID: <aDb5m1tfJSg.WdlUcxDgAgAJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/aDb5m1tfJSg/m/WdlUcxDgAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi,
I'm trying to pass a string as a variable name to `select()`. It works fine with `select_()`, however, now it is deprecated.
I'm not sure how to do it with the new way `select` is suppose to work.

df <- tibble(a = 1:5, b = 6:10)
var1 <- "a"

either of  this works fine:

df %>% select(a)
df %>% select_(var1)

and this doesn't work:

df %>% select(var1) # I knew this already would not work

# Error: var1: must resolve to integer column positions, not string

df %>% select(!!var1)

# Error: "a": must resolve to integer column positions, not string

df %>% select(UQ(var1))

# Error: "a": must resolve to integer column positions, not string

Honestly, I'm still struggling a little in wrapping my head around standard and non standard evaluation.

What is the proper approach to solve this issue?

Thanks in advance!

Massimo

From ub756a7daa2c2aa6d at groups.google.com  Tue Apr 18 22:12:53 2017
From: ub756a7daa2c2aa6d at groups.google.com (Hadley Wickham)
Date: Tue, 18 Apr 2017 22:12:53 +0000
Subject: Re: dplyr_0.5.0.9002, passing string as variable name to select
Message-ID: <aDb5m1tfJSg.RC4ODsrwAgAJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <aDb5m1tfJSg.WdlUcxDgAgAJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
References: <aDb5m1tfJSg.WdlUcxDgAgAJ@manipulatr.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/manipulatr/c/aDb5m1tfJSg/m/RC4ODsrwAgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You've got two options:

1. Convert the string to a symbol:

df %>% select(!!sym(var1))

1. Use the existing one_of() helper

df %>% select(one_of(var))

Hadley

